For each integer $p\geq3$, set $s_p(n)=p8^n-4^n-2^n$ for $n\geq0$.
These positive integer sequences have rank three and all satisfy
\begin{equation}
 s_p(n+3)=14s_p(n+2)-56s_p(n+1)+64s_p(n).
\end{equation}
Write $R_p=R_{s_p}$. Then
\begin{equation}
 R_p(M)=\#\{0\leq j\leq2M:[X^j](1+X-pX^2)^M\ne0\}.
\end{equation}
For each fixed $M\geq1$, $R_p(M)=2M+1$ for all but finitely many
integers $p\geq3$, but $R_p(2p+1)\leq4p+2$ for every $p$.
The set $\{R_p:p\geq3\}$ is infinite.

For every integer $n\geq2$, the characteristic polynomial is
\begin{equation}
\det(xI_n-M_n)=x^{n-2}(x^2-T_nx-a_n).
\end{equation}
The matrix $M_n$ has rank two and
\begin{equation}
\tr(\textstyle\bigwedge^k M_n)=
\begin{cases}
1,&k=0,\\ T_n,&k=1,\\ -a_n,&k=2,\\ 0,&k\geq3.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In fact $\bigwedge^k M_n$ is the zero map for every $k\geq3$.

The three reciprocal sums in A322844 are
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k\geq1}\frac1{a_{2k}}&=2+\frac{\pi^2}{6}-\pi\coth(\pi/2),\\
\sum_{k\geq1}\frac1{a_{2k+1}}&=15-\frac{3\pi^2}{2},\\
\sum_{n\geq2}\frac1{a_n}&=17-\frac{4\pi^2}{3}-\pi\coth(\pi/2).
\end{align*}

Write $\eps=n\bmod2$ and
\begin{equation}
 T_n=\frac{n(n^2+\eps)}2,\qquad
 A_n=\frac{n^2}{12}\{3(1+n^2)-2(2+n^2)\eps\}
 =\begin{cases}
 n^2(n^2+1)/4,&n\text{ even},\\
 n^2(n^2-1)/12,&n\text{ odd}.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
For every integer $n\geq2$,
\begin{equation}
 \det(xI-M(n))=x^{n-2}(x^2-T_nx-A_n).
\end{equation}
Consequently $e_1(M(n))=T_n$, $e_2(M(n))=-A_n$, and
$|e_2(M(n))|=A_n=a(n)$ in A322844. For every $n\geq0$ and $k>2$,
$e_k(M(n))=0$.

